Subject: Speed of evolution (was Human Language)

Nancy Lebovitz says:

> I would think that the thing most strongly selected for is the
> willingness/ability to submit (at least superficially) to governments.

  I might make that a little broader.  It seems clear that cooperation confers
survival benefits in many human societies, as well as in societies of many
non-human social animals.  I can well imagine that groups of critters that
did not cooperate effectively were selected against, and also that
uncooperative individuals in basically cooperative groups were selected
against.

  Hmn.  I suspect most present Extropians would advocate cooperation via
negotiation, contracts and market mechanisms.  But certainly, many of the
animals that demonstrate cooperation evolved that behavior without having
the intellectual facilities for these mechanisms.  And quite possibly
cooperation arose in our own ancestors before they had such mechanism.
(I don't suggest certainty, I merely note the presence of lots of
not-too-bright cooperating primates.  Present company excepted. :-)  )

                                          -- Jay Freeman

